-- postpartum week 101: How much vitamin C does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   101,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much vitamin C does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Vitamin C supports your toddler''s immune function, wound healing and the body''s ability to absorb iron from plant foods — and it''s easy to get enough from ordinary fruits and vegetables, since even small amounts add up quickly. It doesn''t prevent colds, but a well-rounded diet is one of the simplest, most reliable things you can offer for everyday health."',
   'Vitamin C supports collagen formation, healthy skin and gums, wound healing, normal immune function and the absorption of iron from plant foods. Recommended amounts differ between national reference systems, so this plan follows the current Indian guidance for your toddler''s age. Such reference values are intended for populations and planning; they are not amounts that parents need to calculate at every meal. Regularly offering vitamin-C-rich fruits and vegetables is the practical goal. The required amount is modest, and regular small servings can contribute substantially. Guava and amla are especially concentrated sources, while citrus fruits, papaya, tomato and capsicum contribute useful amounts across meals. Citrus fruits, guava, papaya, tomato and capsicum (bell pepper) are all reliable, toddler-friendly sources of vitamin C. Guava and amla (Indian gooseberry) are especially concentrated — often several times richer than an orange for the same amount eaten. Amla''s sharp tartness usually makes it easier to offer cooked and mashed, or mixed in a small amount with another food, rather than raw or as a sugary candy. Mosambi (sweet lime) also contributes vitamin C, although the exact amount varies with the fruit, variety, ripeness and serving size. Vitamin C breaks down with heat, and boiling causes the biggest losses because it also leaches into the cooking water; steaming, microwaving or serving a fruit raw keeps much more of it intact. Many good vitamin-C sources — orange segments, ripe papaya, ripe guava, ripe tomato — are naturally soft enough to serve raw once prepared safely, without needing to cook them at all. Where a vitamin-C vegetable does need cooking for safety or texture, a quick steam preserves more of it than a long boil. Vitamin C is necessary for normal immune function, but vitamin C supplements do not prevent the common cold in most people. Research on regular higher-dose supplements has found slightly shorter colds in some groups, but that does not mean a toddler needs a supplement. Starting vitamin C after cold symptoms appear has not reliably shortened the illness. For everyday nourishment, its most practical additional benefit is helping the body absorb iron from plant foods when the two are offered at the same meal. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals naturally include dairy or eggs.',
   'A note on very restricted diets: if your toddler goes through an extended stretch — weeks or more — of eating almost no fruits or vegetables at all, especially as part of a very narrow or sensory-driven eating pattern, mention this at a routine visit so it can be looked at as part of the overall picture. This is different from an ordinary phase of picky eating that still includes some fruits or vegetables. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s vitamin C intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed — this week''s focus on your toddler''s vitamin C intake doesn''t change your own treatment. A toddler who regularly eats vitamin-C-rich fruits or vegetables is likely obtaining useful amounts. Keep offering a variety across the week rather than measuring every serving or relying on one particular food.',
   '——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– Where a meat, fish or egg option appears, it is optional — choose the vegetarian alternative freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken and mutton boneless and either finely shredded or finely minced with any bone fragments removed, and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked; this week''s fish choice (basa) is optional too. None of these foods are meaningful vitamin C sources — this week''s fruits and vegetables are what cover the need, regardless of which protein option you choose. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin C supports collagen formation, healthy skin and gums, wound healing, normal immune function and the absorption of iron from plant foods. Recommended amounts differ between national reference systems, so this plan follows the current Indian guidance for your toddler''s age. Such reference values are intended for populations and planning; they are not amounts that parents need to calculate at every meal. Regularly offering vitamin-C-rich fruits and vegetables is the practical goal. The required amount is modest, and regular small servings can contribute substantially. Guava and amla are especially concentrated sources, while citrus fruits, papaya, tomato and capsicum contribute useful amounts across meals.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and ripe tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and ripe tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya or finely chopped orange pulp, with seeds and tough membrane removed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources, and the amla exception", "notes": "Citrus fruits, guava, papaya, tomato and capsicum (bell pepper) are all reliable, toddler-friendly sources of vitamin C. Guava and amla (Indian gooseberry) are especially concentrated — often several times richer than an orange for the same amount eaten. Amla''s sharp tartness usually makes it easier to offer cooked and mashed, or mixed in a small amount with another food, rather than raw or as a sugary candy. Mosambi (sweet lime) also contributes vitamin C, although the exact amount varies with the fruit, variety, ripeness and serving size.", "breakfast_a": "Dhokla with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Dhokla with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with guava pulp, deseeded and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred in Day 2 — continued", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked pumpkin, finished with a small amount of cooked, mashed amla according to taste", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin, finished with a small amount of cooked, mashed amla according to taste", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Cooking, and what keeps vitamin C intact", "notes": "Vitamin C breaks down with heat, and boiling causes the biggest losses because it also leaches into the cooking water; steaming, microwaving or serving a fruit raw keeps much more of it intact. Many good vitamin-C sources — orange segments, ripe papaya, ripe guava, ripe tomato — are naturally soft enough to serve raw once prepared safely, without needing to cook them at all. Where a vitamin-C vegetable does need cooking for safety or texture, a quick steam preserves more of it than a long boil.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Oats idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and ripe tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and ripe tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of fennel (saunf) powder", "dinner_a": "Rice with well-mashed rajma and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Immune support, the cold myth, and iron pairing", "notes": "Vitamin C is necessary for normal immune function, but vitamin C supplements do not prevent the common cold in most people. Research on regular higher-dose supplements has found slightly shorter colds in some groups, but that does not mean a toddler needs a supplement. Starting vitamin C after cold symptoms appear has not reliably shortened the illness. For everyday nourishment, its most practical additional benefit is helping the body absorb iron from plant foods when the two are offered at the same meal.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal uttapam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with masoor dal and mustard greens (sarson), cooked until very soft and finely chopped, with tomato stirred through after cooking", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and mustard greens (sarson), cooked until very soft and finely chopped, with tomato stirred through after cooking", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with very ripe custard apple (sitaphal), every seed carefully removed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed and mixed through", "dinner_a": "Rice with chana dal and drumstick leaves (moringa), cooked until very soft and finely chopped, finished with a squeeze of lemon", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and drumstick leaves (moringa), cooked until very soft and finely chopped, finished with a squeeze of lemon", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Rare, but worth knowing", "notes": "Vitamin C deficiency is rare, but it can occur after a prolonged period of extremely restricted eating. Seek medical assessment promptly if a toddler with a very limited diet develops unexplained leg pain or limping, swollen or bleeding gums, unusual bruising, marked tiredness or increasing difficulty eating. These symptoms can have several causes and should not be diagnosed or treated with supplements at home.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable stuffed paratha with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable stuffed paratha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side Day 5 — continued", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (padwal), finely chopped, with tomato stirred through after cooking", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked snake gourd (padwal), finely chopped, with tomato stirred through after cooking", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a fresh fig (anjeer), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted coriander (dhania) powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Safety, supplements, and a gentle habit", "notes": "Vitamin C from ordinary food is unlikely to cause excessive intake. In US guidance, the tolerable upper intake level for children aged one to three is 400 milligrams a day from all sources combined — including food, drinks and supplements. High supplemental doses can cause diarrhoea, nausea or stomach cramps. A dedicated vitamin C supplement is usually unnecessary for a toddler eating a reasonably varied diet; use one only when recommended for that child by a qualified healthcare professional. A gummy''s candy-like taste can also lead a toddler to eat more than intended, and many are sweet and slightly acidic, which is worth keeping in mind for dental health. Keep all vitamins and supplements locked away and out of sight and reach — gummy vitamins can be mistaken for sweets and may also present a choking risk if their size or texture is unsuitable.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with ripe litchi flesh, with the seed completely removed, cut into very small pieces and served while the toddler is seated and supervised", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pat of ghee", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped, finished with a squeeze of lemon", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and capsicum, lightly steamed and finely chopped, finished with a squeeze of lemon", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did your toddler get some vitamin-C-rich fruit or vegetable regularly — and did pairing it with a dal or leafy-green dish become a habit worth keeping?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Vitamin C is one of the easier nutrients in this series — small amounts, ordinary foods, and a genuine bonus for iron absorption when paired thoughtfully at the same meal.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 102: How much omega-3 does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   102,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much omega-3 does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Omega-3 fats — found in ground flaxseed, finely ground walnuts, fully soaked chia seeds, oily fish such as sardines and Indian mackerel, and some fortified foods — matter as part of your toddler''s ongoing nourishment. There is no single agreed EPA or DHA target for toddlers, and extra supplementation has not been shown to sharpen the thinking of a typically developing child. Offering suitable foods regularly is the practical, food-first approach."',
   'Omega-3 fats include ALA, EPA and DHA. ALA is an essential fat found in foods such as ground flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts and certain plant oils. US guidance uses an Adequate Intake of 0.7 grams of ALA a day for children aged one to three, but this is a population reference — not an amount families need to calculate every day. Specific toddler requirements for EPA and DHA have not been established. Oily fish provides EPA and DHA directly, while plant foods provide ALA. The practical goal is to offer suitable omega-3-containing foods as part of a varied diet rather than chase an exact number or assume that every toddler needs a supplement. Ground flaxseed, walnuts, chia seeds and soybean oil are useful plant sources of ALA. For the longer-chain DHA and EPA, oily fish are the richest source — and among Indian fish, this varies more than people expect: sardines (tarli) and Indian mackerel (bangda) are considerably richer in omega-3 than more commonly eaten fish like rohu or catla. For toddlers, walnuts should be ground to a fine powder and mixed thoroughly into soft food, or offered as a very thin layer of smooth walnut butter spread across soft bread or roti. Never offer whole nuts, coarse nut pieces, or a spoonful or thick lump of nut butter. Plant foods provide ALA, while fish and algae provide DHA and EPA directly. The body can convert some ALA into EPA and DHA, but conversion is limited and varies between individuals. That does not mean a vegetarian toddler is automatically deficient or requires DHA drops, especially because no universally accepted toddler DHA target has been established. Continue offering varied plant sources, and discuss the child''s complete diet with a qualified healthcare professional if the family follows a strictly vegan diet or has other nutritional concerns. DHA is an important structural component of the brain and retina, which explains why it receives considerable attention during pregnancy and infancy. Its normal biological role does not mean that giving extra DHA will make a typically developing toddler more intelligent or improve learning. Research has not established a clear cognitive benefit from routinely supplementing otherwise healthy toddlers. Offer nutritious food sources without presenting them as a treatment or a way to enhance intelligence. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals naturally include dairy or eggs.',
   'A note on strictly vegan diets: a well-planned vegan diet can provide ALA through ground seeds, walnuts and suitable plant oils. Because vegan toddlers also require attention to nutrients such as vitamin B12, iron, iodine, calcium and vitamin D, discuss the overall eating pattern at a routine health visit. An algae-based DHA supplement should be considered only as an individual decision with a qualified healthcare professional — not as an automatic requirement for every vegan toddler. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s omega-3 intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed — this week''s focus on your toddler''s omega-3 intake doesn''t change your own treatment. Regularly offering suitable ALA sources — such as finely ground flaxseed, fully soaked chia or finely ground walnut — and, where the family eats fish, occasional age-appropriate lower-mercury oily fish provides a practical food-first approach. Exact intake does not need to be calculated meal by meal.',
   '——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– Sardines and Indian mackerel are the principal oily-fish omega-3 sources in this week''s chart — basa is a leaner fish and isn''t used this week. This chart includes two small toddler fish meals; fish guidance differs by country and species, so vary the species and follow current Indian or local advice. All meat, fish and egg options remain optional — choose the vegetarian alternative freely. Cook everything through, keep chicken and mutton boneless and finely shredded or minced with bone fragments removed, and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Omega-3 fats include ALA, EPA and DHA. ALA is an essential fat found in foods such as ground flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts and certain plant oils. US guidance uses an Adequate Intake of 0.7 grams of ALA a day for children aged one to three, but this is a population reference — not an amount families need to calculate every day. Specific toddler requirements for EPA and DHA have not been established. Oily fish provides EPA and DHA directly, while plant foods provide ALA. The practical goal is to offer suitable omega-3-containing foods as part of a varied diet rather than chase an exact number or assume that every toddler needs a supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin, mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin, mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with one teaspoon of finely ground flaxseed stirred thoroughly through", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources, and an Indian-fish surprise", "notes": "Ground flaxseed, walnuts, chia seeds and soybean oil are useful plant sources of ALA. For the longer-chain DHA and EPA, oily fish are the richest source — and among Indian fish, this varies more than people expect: sardines (tarli) and Indian mackerel (bangda) are considerably richer in omega-3 than more commonly eaten fish like rohu or catla. For toddlers, walnuts should be ground to a fine powder and mixed thoroughly into soft food, or offered as a very thin layer of smooth walnut butter spread across soft bread or roti. Never offer whole nuts, coarse nut pieces, or a spoonful or thick lump of nut butter.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a mild Indian mackerel (bangda) curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely chopped Day 2 — continued", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with walnut ground to a fine powder and stirred thoroughly through", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred in", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Plant and marine sources are different", "notes": "Plant foods provide ALA, while fish and algae provide DHA and EPA directly. The body can convert some ALA into EPA and DHA, but conversion is limited and varies between individuals. That does not mean a vegetarian toddler is automatically deficient or requires DHA drops, especially because no universally accepted toddler DHA target has been established. Continue offering varied plant sources, and discuss the child''s complete diet with a qualified healthcare professional if the family follows a strictly vegan diet or has other nutritional concerns.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild sardine (tarli) curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small toddler portion of chia pudding made with curd, chia seeds fully soaked until uniformly soft and gelled", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of fennel (saunf) powder", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What the evidence really shows", "notes": "DHA is an important structural component of the brain and retina, which explains why it receives considerable attention during pregnancy and infancy. Its normal biological role does not mean that giving extra DHA will make a typically developing toddler more intelligent or improve learning. Research has not established a clear cognitive benefit from routinely supplementing otherwise healthy toddlers. Offer nutritious food sources without presenting them as a treatment or a way to enhance intelligence.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with masoor dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and one teaspoon of finely ground flaxseed stirred thoroughly through", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed and mixed through", "dinner_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely grated", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Supplements, and when to ask your doctor", "notes": "Most toddlers do not need an omega-3 supplement merely because such products are marketed for brain development. Fish-oil, algae-oil and fish-liver-oil products can contain different ingredients and widely different doses. Product quality also varies. Use an age-appropriate supplement only when recommended for that child by a qualified healthcare professional, and ask about the dose, product quality and all added vitamins. Do not substitute a product intended for adults, and keep every supplement locked away and out of sight and reach. Fish-liver oils deserve particular caution because they may contain vitamins A and D in addition to omega-3.", "breakfast_a": "Oats dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Oats dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side Day 5 — continued", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked drumstick (pulp only, fibrous pod removed)", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick (pulp only, fibrous pod removed)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a very thin layer of smooth walnut butter spread across a small piece of soft roti", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pat of ghee", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked bhindi, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bhindi, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Keeping it simple", "notes": "You may hear about balancing \"omega-6 and omega-3\" in the diet, but major health bodies say there''s no proven ideal ratio to aim for once both are present in reasonable amounts — it isn''t something worth tracking closely. Mercury risk depends on the exact fish species, its size and where it was caught. Use clearly identified, lower-mercury fish suitable for young children, vary the species and follow current national or local advisories. \"Mackerel\" is not one uniform category, so specify Indian mackerel rather than using the general term. Carefully remove every bone before serving.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with walnut ground to a fine powder and mashed ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of hing (asafoetida)", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tomato, skin and seeds removed, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did some walnut, flaxseed or oily fish make it onto your toddler''s plate a few times — without needing to track an exact amount?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Omega-3 is worth taking seriously without turning it into a number to chase — suitable foods offered regularly support the goal, while supplements remain an individual decision rather than a routine requirement.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 103: How much vitamin A does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   103,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much vitamin A does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Vitamin A supports your toddler''s vision (especially in low light), immune function and skin health, and comes from two dietary sources: preformed vitamin A in animal foods, and provitamin A carotenoids in colorful fruits and vegetables. Ordinary variety among the safe foods your family uses matters more than chasing a number — and in India, that also means keeping your child''s scheduled national vitamin A doses on track."',
   'Vitamin A recommendations account for the fact that preformed vitamin A and plant carotenoids are used differently by the body. National authorities may use different units and conversion conventions, so their figures should not be compared without standardising those units first. Families do not need to calculate micrograms at each meal. Follow current Indian guidance, offer a varied diet and keep your child''s nationally scheduled vitamin A doses up to date. Vitamin A comes from two broad dietary sources. Preformed vitamin A is found in foods such as liver, egg yolk and dairy products. Provitamin A carotenoids are found in foods such as carrot, pumpkin, sweet potato, spinach, mango and papaya, and the body can convert them into vitamin A. Different carotenoids have different conversion rates. In the US RAE system, 12 micrograms of dietary beta-carotene or 24 micrograms of dietary alpha-carotene or beta-cryptoxanthin is counted as 1 microgram RAE. These are accounting conversions — not a reason to treat plant foods as inferior or to calculate every meal. Egg yolk and dairy are useful, moderate sources of preformed vitamin A. For provitamin A carotenoids, carrot, pumpkin, spinach, mango and papaya are all valuable — remember from yesterday that carotenoid amounts are simply counted using a different accounting conversion, not because plant foods are inferior. Cooking and mashing carrot and pumpkin, rather than serving them raw in chunks, also helps the body absorb more of what''s there, alongside being the safer texture for a toddler. Liver is exceptionally concentrated in preformed vitamin A — a small, toddler-sized serving can contain several times a whole day''s worth. This makes liver a food worth offering only occasionally and in a small amount, rather than as a regular rotation item, since repeated servings can add up to more than a toddler''s body should handle. This caution is specific to liver and other concentrated animal sources — it doesn''t apply to vegetables or fruit, which carry a different safety profile, as later this week explains. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals naturally include dairy or eggs.',
   'India''s national vitamin A programme: Children aged 9–59 months are covered by India''s National Vitamin A Supplementation Programme. The first dose is ordinarily given at 9 months, followed by further scheduled doses up to 5 years of age. Check your child''s Mother and Child Protection Card or ask your local health worker whether the next dose is due. Follow the national schedule and any clinician-directed treatment, but do not add an over-the-counter vitamin A, retinol or cod-liver-oil product on your own. Tell the healthcare professional when your child last received vitamin A so that doses are not accidentally duplicated. A note on very limited diets: if your toddler has very limited access to dairy, eggs, or a variety of vegetables and fruit, or eats a very narrow range of foods over a long period, mention this at a routine visit so it can be assessed early. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s vitamin A intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed — this week''s focus on your toddler''s vitamin A intake doesn''t change your own treatment. Regularly offering varied sources — including colourful vegetables and fruit, and egg or dairy where used — supports vitamin A intake alongside your child''s scheduled public-health care.',
   '—————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– Egg yolk is a useful, moderate vitamin A source and appears through the week as an optional side; vegetarian choices cover carotenoid-based vitamin A well through the vegetable and fruit options. Liver is extremely concentrated in preformed vitamin A and is not needed as a routine toddler food — it isn''t a regular chart option this week for that reason. If your family chooses to offer it, follow current age-appropriate national guidance and avoid combining it with additional retinol-containing supplements unless advised by a healthcare professional. A mother who is pregnant or could be pregnant should avoid liver, liver products, cod-liver oil and supplements containing retinol unless her maternity team specifically advises otherwise. Cook all meat, fish and eggs the way through, keep chicken and mutton boneless and finely shredded or minced with bone fragments removed, and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin A recommendations account for the fact that preformed vitamin A and plant carotenoids are used differently by the body. National authorities may use different units and conversion conventions, so their figures should not be compared without standardising those units first. Families do not need to calculate micrograms at each meal. Follow current Indian guidance, offer a varied diet and keep your child''s nationally scheduled vitamin A doses up to date.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe mango or papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Two different forms", "notes": "Vitamin A comes from two broad dietary sources. Preformed vitamin A is found in foods such as liver, egg yolk and dairy products. Provitamin A carotenoids are found in foods such as carrot, pumpkin, sweet potato, spinach, mango and papaya, and the body can convert them into vitamin A. Different carotenoids have different conversion rates. In the US RAE system, 12 micrograms of dietary beta-carotene or 24 micrograms of dietary alpha-carotene or beta-cryptoxanthin is counted as 1 microgram RAE. These are accounting conversions — not a reason to treat plant foods as inferior or to calculate every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and pumpkin (kaddu), soft-cooked and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and pumpkin (kaddu), soft-cooked and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred in Day 2 — continued", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Everyday food sources", "notes": "Egg yolk and dairy are useful, moderate sources of preformed vitamin A. For provitamin A carotenoids, carrot, pumpkin, spinach, mango and papaya are all valuable — remember from yesterday that carotenoid amounts are simply counted using a different accounting conversion, not because plant foods are inferior. Cooking and mashing carrot and pumpkin, rather than serving them raw in chunks, also helps the body absorb more of what''s there, alongside being the safer texture for a toddler.", "breakfast_a": "Paneer paratha with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Paneer paratha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with finely chopped ripe muskmelon (kharbuja)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of fennel (saunf) powder", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why liver needs restraint", "notes": "Liver is exceptionally concentrated in preformed vitamin A — a small, toddler-sized serving can contain several times a whole day''s worth. This makes liver a food worth offering only occasionally and in a small amount, rather than as a regular rotation item, since repeated servings can add up to more than a toddler''s body should handle. This caution is specific to liver and other concentrated animal sources — it doesn''t apply to vegetables or fruit, which carry a different safety profile, as later this week explains.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed soft-cooked pumpkin and a pinch of cinnamon, if tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed and mixed through", "dinner_a": "Rice with chana dal and pumpkin (kaddu), soft-cooked and mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, meticulously deboned and finely flaked, and pumpkin (kaddu), soft-cooked and mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Deficiency, and where it still matters", "notes": "Vitamin A deficiency can affect vision, beginning with difficulty seeing in low light, and prolonged deficiency can cause serious eye damage. Risk is greater in children with severe dietary restriction, undernutrition, fat-malabsorption conditions or limited access to varied foods. Seek medical assessment if you are concerned about vision, eye dryness, poor growth or a persistently restricted diet. Do not attempt to diagnose or treat deficiency with high-dose supplements at home. Children under five with suspected or diagnosed measles may receive two age-specific vitamin A doses under medical supervision. This is recognised treatment for measles and is separate from routine dietary advice and scheduled prevention doses. Do not copy the dose from the internet or give additional vitamin A at home unless instructed by the treating healthcare professional.", "breakfast_a": "Suji uttapam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and spinach (palak), cooked until very soft and finely chopped Day 5 — continued", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pat of ghee", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The carotenemia myth, and keeping supplements locked away", "notes": "Provitamin A carotenoids from ordinary fruits and vegetables are not associated with the same toxicity risk as excessive preformed vitamin A. Eating a lot of carrot, pumpkin, mango or similar produce can turn a toddler''s skin slightly yellow-orange (carotenemia), most visible on the palms and soles. Carotenemia from food is generally harmless, but it is still sensible to offer variety rather than unusually large amounts of one food every day. Carotenemia does not usually yellow the whites of the eyes. Seek medical advice promptly if the eye whites are yellow, or if the colour change is accompanied by dark urine, pale stools, vomiting, unusual sleepiness, poor feeding or illness, because those signs should not be assumed to come from carrots or pumpkin. Excessive preformed vitamin A from supplements can be harmful. Keep vitamin products, cod-liver oil and adult multivitamins locked away and out of sight and reach. Follow India''s scheduled child vitamin A programme and any clinician-prescribed treatment, but do not give extra over-the-counter vitamin A at the same time unless a qualified healthcare professional confirms that it is appropriate. Always report the date of the child''s most recent scheduled dose.", "breakfast_a": "Upma with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and sweet potato (shakarkandi), soft-cooked and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of ajwain (carom seed) powder", "dinner_a": "Rice with rajma, well mashed, and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments, and carrot, finely grated and cooked until soft", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did colourful vegetables or fruit appear regularly, with egg or dairy where your family uses them — and is your child''s nationally scheduled vitamin A record up to date?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Vitamin A is supported by ordinary dietary variety and appropriate public-health care. Colourful vegetables and fruit can be offered regularly as part of a varied, balanced diet, while liver, retinol-containing supplements and scheduled high-dose vitamin A each require different safety rules.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 104: Eating well as your toddler turns two
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   104,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as your toddler turns two',
   '"Two years in, the number on any chart still matters less than the steady, unglamorous work of showing up with food, again and again. That work is the whole story."',
   'Around the second birthday, standing height commonly replaces lying-down length. Standing height is naturally a little lower than recumbent length, and the WHO growth standards account for this measurement difference. A single small shift should therefore be interpreted alongside your child''s longer-term growth pattern rather than treated as evidence that something has suddenly changed. Continued breastfeeding remains valuable at two years and beyond for as long as it works for you both. If your child drinks cow''s milk, full-fat milk is commonly used through the second year. After two, a gradual move towards toned or another appropriate lower-fat milk may be reasonable when a child is growing well and eating a varied diet. A child with poor growth, a very limited diet or higher energy needs may need to continue with full-fat milk, based on individual guidance. The everyday foundations do not change abruptly at two: regular family meals containing grains or other energy-giving foods, vegetables and fruit, and an iron- and protein-rich food such as dal, beans, paneer, egg, fish or meat. Include dairy or an appropriate alternative where your family uses it, and continue responding to the child''s hunger and fullness rather than enforcing fixed proportions. Chewing ability, cup and fork use, and vocabulary all continue developing well past this birthday. Some two-year-olds manage an open cup and fork with little spillage, while others are still practising, and a wide range of progress is common. Persistent difficulty chewing or swallowing, frequent coughing or choking, loss of a previously acquired skill, or distress with ordinary textures is worth raising with a healthcare professional. A fast-growing vocabulary increasingly lets your toddler tell you ''more,'' ''all done,'' or which food they want, rather than you having to guess. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If you''re feeling emotional about this birthday: it''s common to feel a mix of pride and a little grief that the baby stage is ending — both are worth making room for, and neither needs to be resolved this week. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be a bigger week than usual. Steady meals, a good question or two saved for your child''s healthcare provider, and a moment to notice how far you''ve both come is genuinely enough.',
   '——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and egg remain optional throughout the week. Choose either meal option according to your family''s dietary pattern — vegetarian meals do not need an additional non-vegetarian food. Cook eggs, meat and fish fully; remove every bone fragment; keep chicken and mutton soft and finely shredded or minced; and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Around the second birthday, standing height commonly replaces lying-down length. Standing height is naturally a little lower than recumbent length, and the WHO growth standards account for this measurement difference. A single small shift should therefore be interpreted alongside your child''s longer-term growth pattern rather than treated as evidence that something has suddenly changed. This week is a checkpoint more than a challenge: what actually changes on the plate at two (less than you''d think), what''s new developmentally, and what''s worth asking your child''s healthcare provider at this particular visit. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability and supervise all eating closely — choking precautions don''t ease up at this birthday, more on that on Day 4.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Oats porridge with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "lunch_a": "Tomato rice with soft-cooked green beans", "lunch_b": "Tomato rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Apple, peeled and very finely grated or gently softened, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a boneless, finely shredded chicken curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The milk conversation", "notes": "Continued breastfeeding remains valuable at two years and beyond for as long as it works for you both. If your child drinks cow''s milk, full-fat milk is commonly used through the second year. After two, a gradual move towards toned or another appropriate lower-fat milk may be reasonable when a child is growing well and eating a varied diet. A child with poor growth, a very limited diet or higher energy needs may need to continue with full-fat milk, based on individual guidance. Milk can be part of the day without becoming the whole day. Large amounts can crowd out meals and iron-rich foods, so offer it alongside a varied diet rather than repeatedly in place of food. Prefer a cup, avoid putting the child to sleep with a milk bottle, and do not add sugar, sweetened powders or flavouring syrups.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Moong dal khichdi with soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Moong dal khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed sweet potato with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated cucumber", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a paneer bhurji, finely crumbled", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a fully cooked egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What actually changed on the plate", "notes": "The everyday foundations do not change abruptly at two: regular family meals containing grains or other energy-giving foods, vegetables and fruit, and an iron- and protein-rich food such as dal, beans, paneer, egg, fish or meat. Include dairy or an appropriate alternative where your family uses it, and continue responding to the child''s hunger and fullness rather than enforcing fixed proportions. For your child: This is a good week to keep things exactly as steady and unremarkable as they''ve been — no overhaul needed, just the same rhythm of family meals continuing forward.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya upma", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked ridge gourd and moong dal with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked ridge gourd and rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken Day 3 — continued", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe papaya, cut into small pieces or mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with pasteurised cheese, finely grated or crumbled", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "New skills at the table", "notes": "Chewing ability, cup and fork use, and vocabulary all continue developing well past this birthday. Some two-year-olds manage an open cup and fork with little spillage, while others are still practising, and a wide range of progress is common. Persistent difficulty chewing or swallowing, frequent coughing or choking, loss of a previously acquired skill, or distress with ordinary textures is worth raising with a healthcare professional. A fast-growing vocabulary increasingly lets your toddler tell you ''more,'' ''all done,'' or which food they want, rather than you having to guess. Worth knowing: Choking precautions do not end at two. Continue cutting grapes and cherry tomatoes lengthwise into small quarters, avoid whole nuts and popcorn, spread nut or seed butter thinly, soften or finely grate hard raw fruit and vegetables, grate or finely crumble firm cheese, remove every bone fragment, and prepare meat in a soft texture your child can manage. Keep your child seated and supervise every meal and snack.", "breakfast_a": "Suji cheela with grated vegetables and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji cheela with grated vegetables and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked cabbage and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked cabbage and potato sabzi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked carrot, finely mashed or grated", "nourishment_break_b": "A very thin spread of smooth peanut butter on soft toast fingers, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Two, and full of opinions", "notes": "A stronger sense of ''no,'' a sudden refusal of a food they loved last week, or sticking to just one or two familiar things for a while — all of this is developmentally expected right around now, not a sign that mealtimes have gone wrong. The same approach that''s worked so far still applies: you decide what''s offered, when, and where; your child decides whether and how much. For your child: Offering a bounded choice — ''peas or carrots tonight?'' — respects your child''s growing sense of independence without turning the whole meal into a negotiation.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a smooth, mild chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lentil dal and drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "lunch_b": "Rice with finely minced chicken, fully cooked, and drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "nourishment_break_a": "Grated cucumber with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed papaya", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a fully cooked egg curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The two-year checkup", "notes": "Your local checkup schedule around this age may include a nutrition discussion alongside routine screening — commonly touching on meal and snack frequency, the milk conversation, picky eating and choking prevention, though the exact screening panel (hearing, vision, lead, development and so on) varies by country, health system and individual risk factors. None of it needs to be a source of worry going in; it''s simply a scheduled check-in built for exactly this stage. For your child: If anything about your child''s eating has felt off for a while now — not just a picky few days but a persistently narrow diet, ongoing distress at meals, coughing or choking during meals, difficulty swallowing, recurrent vomiting, dehydration, weight or growth concerns, or a feeding skill that seems to have gone backward rather than forward — this visit is the right place to raise it.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia with pasteurised cheese, finely grated and stirred in", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia with a fully cooked egg, finely mashed and stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked carrot and peas curry with khichdi", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked carrot and peas curry with boneless, finely shredded chicken and khichdi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with apple, peeled and very finely grated or gently softened", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft, ripe pear, peeled and very finely grated, with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a lightly spiced fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back over the last two years of feeding this particular child, what''s one thing you''re proud you stuck with — and one thing you''re ready to let go of?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Two years of meals, most of them entirely unremarkable, have quietly built the child in front of you — what would it feel like to let this birthday be a celebration of that ordinary, repeated effort, rather than a test to pass?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 105: How much choline does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   105,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much choline does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Choline helps form cell membranes and supports normal liver and nervous-system function. It also has an established biological role in brain development, although researchers are still studying whether giving extra choline beyond an adequate diet improves later learning or memory. Because the current Indian guidance reviewed for this programme does not provide a separate toddler target, the practical goal is varied food—not chasing a supplement or a daily number."',
   'Choline helps form the membranes surrounding cells and contributes to nervous-system function and the transport of fat from the liver. The body can make some choline, but food supplies the remainder. Severe deficiency can affect the liver and muscles, although clear deficiency is uncommon and has not been well characterised in healthy toddlers eating varied diets. The current WHO and ICMR-NIN guidance reviewed for this programme does not provide a separate daily choline target for Indian toddlers. Other authorities use Adequate Intake figures rather than a precisely measured requirement: the United States uses 200 mg a day for ages one to three, while the European Food Safety Authority uses 140 mg a day. These figures were derived using different methods and should not be treated as competing prescriptions for an individual child. Families do not need to calculate choline at every meal. Egg yolk is a concentrated everyday source of choline. Milk, curd and other dairy foods contribute smaller amounts. Where a family uses them, egg and dairy can contribute meaningfully alongside dal, legumes, grains and vegetables—without requiring the parent to calculate a daily total. Dal, rajma, chana and soy foods are useful plant sources of choline, alongside vegetables such as cauliflower, peas, spinach and cabbage. A vegetarian toddler who eats dairy plus a reasonable variety of dal, legumes and vegetables typically has many contributing sources across the day. A fully vegan toddler — with no animal-derived foods at all — needs more deliberate planning, since egg and dairy are excluded; more on that below. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If your toddler eats no animal-derived foods: include varied legumes and grains, with regular soy foods such as soft tofu or well-cooked soybeans where soy has already been introduced and tolerated. Vegetables including cauliflower, peas, spinach and cabbage make additional contributions but should not be presented as substitutes for more concentrated sources on their own. A persistently narrow vegan diet deserves individual nutritional assessment — not because vegan feeding is inherently inadequate, but because several nutrients require deliberate planning at this age. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. Regularly offering a variety of these foods supports choline intake without requiring special products or daily calculations.',
   '——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– Egg yolk is a concentrated everyday source of choline and appears through the week as an optional side or mixed-in ingredient. Vegetarian meals include several contributing sources through dal, legumes, paneer and dairy and do not require an added non-vegetarian food. Liver is also very high in choline, though it is not included as a chart option this week, since it is better offered only occasionally rather than as a regular rotation item. Cook eggs, meat and fish fully; remove every bone fragment; keep chicken and mutton soft and finely shredded or minced; and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Choline helps form the membranes surrounding cells and contributes to nervous-system function and the transport of fat from the liver. The body can make some choline, but food supplies the remainder. Severe deficiency can affect the liver and muscles, although clear deficiency is uncommon and has not been well characterised in healthy toddlers eating varied diets.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked peas (matar), mashed with finely crumbled paneer", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of asafoetida (hing)", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki)", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "How much, and why there''s no single number", "notes": "The current WHO and ICMR-NIN guidance reviewed for this programme does not provide a separate daily choline target for Indian toddlers. Other authorities use Adequate Intake figures rather than a precisely measured requirement: the United States uses 200 mg a day for ages one to three, while the European Food Safety Authority uses 140 mg a day. These figures were derived using different methods and should not be treated as competing prescriptions for an individual child. Families do not need to calculate choline at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, and soft-cooked peas (matar)", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked peas (matar)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked sweet potato (shakarkandi), mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed and stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a fully cooked egg curry", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Egg and dairy — the most concentrated everyday sources", "notes": "Egg yolk is a concentrated everyday source of choline. Milk, curd and other dairy foods contribute smaller amounts. Where a family uses them, egg and dairy can contribute meaningfully alongside dal, legumes, grains and vegetables—without requiring the parent to calculate a daily total.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal chilla with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped Day 3 — continued", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a fully cooked egg bhurji, finely mashed and stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a paneer bhurji, finely crumbled", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a fully cooked egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Plant sources, for vegetarian and vegan families", "notes": "Dal, rajma, chana and soy foods are useful plant sources of choline, alongside vegetables such as cauliflower, peas, spinach and cabbage. A vegetarian toddler who eats dairy plus a reasonable variety of dal, legumes and vegetables typically has many contributing sources across the day. A fully vegan toddler — with no animal-derived foods at all — needs more deliberate planning, since egg and dairy are excluded; more on that below.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable pesarattu with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable pesarattu with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed sweet potato (shakarkandi) with a small amount of smooth peanut butter, spread or mixed thinly, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma sabzi, cooked until very soft and finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Deficiency, supplements, and safety", "notes": "Clear choline deficiency is uncommon, and its signs are not specific enough for a parent to diagnose at home. Do not use a choline supplement to treat tiredness, poor appetite, developmental concerns or suspected fatty liver. Those concerns need medical assessment. High supplemental intakes can cause adverse effects, while ordinary varied meals are unlikely to provide excessive amounts. Do not give a toddler choline, lecithin or another concentrated supplement unless it has been specifically recommended for that child.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Fully ripe guava (amrud) pulp, peeled and carefully strained or cleared of hard seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), mashed with finely crumbled paneer", "dinner_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a lightly spiced fish curry, fully cooked, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "What the newer research does — and doesn''t — show", "notes": "Choline has an established role in normal cell and nervous-system function. What remains uncertain is whether consuming extra choline beyond an adequate diet improves a toddler''s memory, language or intelligence. Research involving pregnancy or animals cannot be translated directly into a promise about a particular toddler. There is therefore no reason to buy a ''brain-development'' supplement or pressure a child to eat a particular food. Offer ordinary variety and let appetite guide how much is eaten. Egg note: most of an egg''s choline is in the yolk. When egg is part of your family''s diet, serve the fully cooked whole egg rather than routinely discarding the yolk for perceived health reasons.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and beetroot (chukandar), finely grated and cooked until soft", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and beetroot (chukandar), finely grated and cooked until soft", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft orange flesh, with peel, seeds and tough membrane removed, separated into very small, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a small pinch of cardamom powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did your child receive a variety of choline-contributing foods — egg or dairy where your family uses them, or soy, dal, legumes, grains and vegetables where it does not?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Choline is one of the newer nutrients to be studied, and its full story — especially around brain development — is still being written. What is already useful is simpler: varied meals containing legumes, grains and vegetables, with egg, dairy or soy where used, can support choline intake without a number to chase or a supplement to buy.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
