-- postpartum week 106: Protecting your toddler's teeth: diet, snacking and cavity prevention
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   106,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Protecting your toddler''s teeth: diet, snacking and cavity prevention',
   '"Both how much free sugar a toddler consumes and how often the teeth are exposed to it influence cavity risk. Keeping sugary foods occasional and offering them with a meal is kinder to teeth than repeated grazing or sipping. The goal is not perfection or shame—it is fewer sugar exposures, plain drinks most of the time, fluoride toothpaste and consistent brushing."',
   'Cavities form when bacteria in the mouth feed on sugars and starches and produce acid, which softens tooth enamel. Saliva normally buffers this acid and helps enamel recover between exposures. A cavity develops when acid attacks happen more often than the mouth can recover from. Baby teeth have thinner enamel than adult teeth, so this process can turn a small mark into a hole within months rather than years — one reason ''it''s just a baby tooth'' is a risky way to think about it. Both the amount and frequency of free sugar matter. Repeated nibbling or sipping gives the teeth less time to recover between acid attacks, so a small sugary food eaten with a meal is generally less harmful than the same food grazed on repeatedly. This does not make unlimited sugar at meals harmless — the useful pattern is smaller amounts, less often, with water and ordinary nourishing foods between meals. After teeth erupt, going to sleep with a bottle or spill-proof cup containing milk, formula, juice or another sweet drink increases cavity risk because the teeth remain exposed while saliva flow is lower. After the bedtime brushing, water is the tooth-friendliest bottle or cup drink. Continued breastfeeding can remain part of family life; mothers do not need to be shamed or told to stop abruptly. If feeding to sleep continues, prioritise twice-daily fluoride brushing, limit other free-sugar exposures and discuss individual risk at the child''s dental visit. A cup used for sipping a sweetened drink continuously through the day works the same way, spread across waking hours instead of the night. Fruit juice counts as sugar for teeth in the same way any sweetened drink does, even when nothing has been added to it — offering the whole fruit instead is the better choice for teeth, not because it has less sugar, but because chewing doesn''t leave sugar sitting on the teeth the way a drink does. Sticky and starchy foods — biscuits, rusk, dried fruit, crackers — cling to tooth surfaces far longer than they seem to, extending the acid exposure well past the time they''re actually eaten. A sip or rinse of plain water after a sweet or starchy snack is a simple, useful follow-up. Brushing itself stays a twice-daily routine — last thing at night and one other time — rather than something to repeat after every snack. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If your toddler already has a visible cavity or tooth pain: see a dentist rather than waiting to see if it resolves on its own — decay in baby teeth can move quickly, and pain or a possible infection is worth prompt attention. If your family''s water comes from a borewell or a known high-fluoride area: use only the recommended rice-grain smear, keep the toothpaste tube out of reach, and encourage spitting as the skill develops — total fluoride intake adds up across water, toothpaste and food. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to become a strict no-sugar week. Keeping free sugars occasional, offering them with meals rather than through repeated grazing, providing plain water between meals and brushing twice daily with the recommended smear of fluoride toothpaste are the practical foundations.',
   '———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish and egg do not need to be added for cavity prevention and remain optional throughout the week. Choose either meal option according to your family''s dietary pattern. Tooth protection comes mainly from limiting free-sugar exposure, avoiding continuous sweet sipping and brushing twice daily with fluoride toothpaste — not from whether a meal is vegetarian or non-vegetarian. Cook eggs, meat and fish fully; remove every bone fragment; keep chicken and mutton soft and finely shredded or minced; and keep fish meticulously deboned and finely flaked. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————-',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Cavities form when bacteria in the mouth feed on sugars and starches and produce acid, which softens tooth enamel. Saliva normally buffers this acid and helps enamel recover between exposures. A cavity develops when acid attacks happen more often than the mouth can recover from. Baby teeth have thinner enamel than adult teeth, so this process can turn a small mark into a hole within months rather than years — one reason ''it''s just a baby tooth'' is a risky way to think about it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra)", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi (okra)", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon, deseeded and cut into small pieces, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of coriander powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan)", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "It''s about timing, not just how much", "notes": "Both the amount and frequency of free sugar matter. Repeated nibbling or sipping gives the teeth less time to recover between acid attacks, so a small sugary food eaten with a meal is generally less harmful than the same food grazed on repeatedly. This does not make unlimited sugar at meals harmless — the useful pattern is smaller amounts, less often, with water and ordinary nourishing foods between meals.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with grated vegetables and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with grated vegetables and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and mixed in Day 2 — continued", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam)", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild basa fish curry, fully cooked, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam)", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The bedtime bottle and the sipping cup", "notes": "After teeth erupt, going to sleep with a bottle or spill-proof cup containing milk, formula, juice or another sweet drink increases cavity risk because the teeth remain exposed while saliva flow is lower. After the bedtime brushing, water is the tooth-friendliest bottle or cup drink. Continued breastfeeding can remain part of family life; mothers do not need to be shamed or told to stop abruptly. If feeding to sleep continues, prioritise twice-daily fluoride brushing, limit other free-sugar exposures and discuss individual risk at the child''s dental visit. A cup used for sipping a sweetened drink continuously through the day works the same way, spread across waking hours instead of the night. Fruit juice counts as sugar for teeth in the same way any sweetened drink does, even when nothing has been added to it — offering the whole fruit instead is the better choice for teeth, not because it has less sugar, but because chewing doesn''t leave sugar sitting on the teeth the way a drink does.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe mango, mashed, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of cumin-coriander powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "The snacks that surprise people", "notes": "Sticky and starchy foods — biscuits, rusk, dried fruit, crackers — cling to tooth surfaces far longer than they seem to, extending the acid exposure well past the time they''re actually eaten. A sip or rinse of plain water after a sweet or starchy snack is a simple, useful follow-up. Brushing itself stays a twice-daily routine — last thing at night and one other time — rather than something to repeat after every snack.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paratha with mixed vegetables and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Stuffed paratha with mixed vegetables and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar)", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked beetroot (chukandar), mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pinch of black pepper", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a boneless, finely shredded chicken curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Brushing, fluoride, and getting the balance right", "notes": "Brush your toddler''s teeth twice daily — last thing at night and on one other occasion — with a soft, child-sized toothbrush and a rice-grain-sized smear of toothpaste containing at least 1,000 ppm fluoride. Check the fluoride concentration on the pack. The parent should perform the effective brushing — a toddler can hold a toothbrush and ''help,'' but plan on doing the actual brushing yourself for several more years. Encourage your child to spit out the toothpaste, but do not rinse with water afterwards, since rinsing washes away the protective fluoride. Worth knowing: If your family uses borewell water or lives in an area known for high natural fluoride, use only the recommended rice-grain smear, keep the toothpaste tube out of reach and do not let the child eat or lick it. Encourage spitting as the skill develops. If the water''s fluoride level is unknown, ask the local water authority, public-health service or a recognised laboratory rather than guessing from taste or appearance. A dentist can interpret your child''s total fluoride exposure.", "breakfast_a": "Suji idli with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and raw banana (kacha kela), soft-cooked and mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and raw banana (kacha kela), soft-cooked and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked carrot, finely grated, mixed into a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a pinch of nutmeg powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton keema, fully cooked, finely minced and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to see a dentist, and what''s actually worth doing", "notes": "A first dental visit is recommended after the first tooth appears and by about the first birthday. If your two-year-old hasn''t been yet, there is no value in guilt — simply arrange a preventive visit now. A dentist can check early changes before they become painful and help you interpret fluoride, feeding and brushing advice for your child. Worth knowing: Arrange a dental assessment for a persistent chalky-white, brown or black area, a visible hole, pain while eating or brushing, ongoing bad breath, bleeding or swollen gums, or a broken tooth. Facial swelling, fever with dental pain, pus, difficulty swallowing or a child who appears unwell requires urgent assessment. Baby teeth aren''t just placeholders. They hold space for the permanent teeth growing underneath and guide how those teeth eventually come in, which is part of why a cavity in a baby tooth is worth treating rather than waiting out. Some cavity-associated bacteria can pass through saliva, so it''s sensible not to share tasting spoons or clean a dropped pacifier in an adult''s mouth. But cavities are not caused by one shared spoon or one caregiver: sugar exposure, fluoride, brushing, enamel, saliva and access to dental care all interact. This guidance is about a helpful habit, not blame.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats chilla with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats chilla with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda)", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda) Day 6 — continued", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe chikoo, mashed, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred through", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. If sweets or sweetened drinks were offered this week, did they mostly happen with a meal rather than through repeated grazing or sipping — and did parent-led brushing happen last thing at night and on one other occasion?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Protecting your toddler''s teeth does not require fear, perfection or shame. Keep sweetened milk, biscuits, juice and mithai occasional rather than routine; offer sugary foods with meals instead of through repeated grazing; provide plain water between meals; and brush twice daily with fluoride toothpaste. These steady habits matter far more than one celebration or one imperfect day.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 107: Food as reward: rethinking dessert, bribery and the clean plate rule
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   107,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Food as reward: rethinking dessert, bribery and the clean plate rule',
   '"Using dessert as the reward for finishing another food, or repeatedly pressuring a toddler to clear their plate, can make mealtimes more stressful and may interfere with their ability to notice hunger and fullness. A calmer approach is to provide regular meals and snacks, include familiar foods alongside newer ones, and allow the child to decide whether and how much to eat."',
   'Using dessert or other foods as a reward for eating, and pressuring a toddler to clear their plate, are very common responses to a parent''s natural worry about intake. Research broadly suggests these habits can add mealtime stress without reliably improving what gets eaten, and may make it harder for a toddler to notice their own hunger and fullness. A calmer approach — offering food without pressure, keeping familiar options nearby, and letting a toddler decide whether and how much to eat — tends to serve both appetite and family harmony better over time. Each day this week unpacks one part of that idea in more detail. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household feels strongly about a finished plate: there''s no need for a confrontation — quietly serving a smaller portion so a cleared plate is easy and genuine, while still leaving room for seconds if your toddler wants more, can honour both. If your toddler repeatedly eats very little, has difficulty chewing or swallowing, loses previously accepted foods, seems unusually tired, or you are concerned about growth, discuss it with their paediatric healthcare professional. Avoid using pressure as the solution. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you manage PCOS or insulin resistance: regular meals containing protein, fibre and minimally processed carbohydrate may support steadier energy; follow any individual treatment advice already given to you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs a dramatic change starting tonight — regular meal times, a dessert that isn''t a bargaining chip, and a plate that''s allowed to end without comment do most of the real work. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about pressure, rewards and portion control applies regardless of what is served. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————-',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "When a food is offered as the reward for eating something else — ''finish your vegetables and you can have dessert'' — research suggests children can end up liking the rewarded food less over time, and the reward food more, even though nothing about the food itself has changed. One explanation researchers have offered is that treating a food as something to be bribed into a child, or withheld as a prize, can shape how desirable it seems. This pattern has mainly been studied where one food is used as a reward for eating another food — praise, attention or a non-food reward alongside repeated tasting appears to work differently, though the evidence here is more limited than for food-based rewards.", "breakfast_a": "Moong sprouts cheela with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong sprouts cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of ajwain (carom seed) powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why pressure and coaxing don''t work the way it feels like they should", "notes": "Pressuring, coaxing or bribing a toddler to eat a disliked food is a very natural response to worry about their diet. In at least one longitudinal study, however, pressuring toddlers to eat disliked foods was not associated with less picky eating a year later, and pressure was more consistently linked with added mealtime conflict and lower enjoyment of meals — for both the child and the parent — without a matching improvement in what was actually eaten.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with finely grated carrot and a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with finely grated carrot and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked parwal (pointed gourd), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked parwal (pointed gourd), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon (kharbuja), deseeded and cut into small pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed and mixed in", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked tomato, deseeded and finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, boiled chickpeas (chole), lightly mashed, and soft-cooked tomato, deseeded and finely chopped", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What the clean plate rule actually teaches", "notes": "Being told to finish everything on the plate can teach a toddler to rely on an external cue — an empty plate — rather than their own sense of fullness, to decide when a meal is over. In classic feeding research, children who were encouraged to clean their plate were more likely to keep eating past the point of fullness simply because food was still available, rather than stopping when they felt satisfied. This doesn''t mean a plate has to be cleared, or left mostly full, to count as a good meal — it means the stopping point is genuinely the toddler''s to decide.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paneer toast with soft seasonal fruit", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable egg toast made with a fully cooked egg, with soft seasonal fruit", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apple, peeled and very finely grated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of amchur (dry mango powder)", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and mixed in", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A simple division: what''s offered, and whether to eat it", "notes": "The approach most often recommended by pediatric and nutrition professionals is a simple division: the adult decides what food is offered, and when and where it''s served; the toddler decides whether to eat it and how much. This isn''t a single proven formula so much as a widely used clinical framework that lines up with the research above — and it doesn''t mean an open kitchen or unlimited treats. Regular meal and snack times, familiar foods offered alongside newer ones, and a calm approach to dessert are all part of the same idea. If dessert is being served, avoid making it conditional on finishing vegetables or clearing the plate. Families may serve it with the meal or at another predictable time. The important shift is that dessert is not used to buy bites of another food.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd)", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pear, peeled and finely grated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pat of ghee", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal and a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What actually helps: repeated, low-key tastes", "notes": "Children often need to meet a food repeatedly before it becomes familiar. There is no single number that applies to every child or every food. Keep offering a small amount occasionally, without pressure, and let looking, touching, smelling or tasting count as part of learning. Even a gentle ''just try one bite'' can feel like pressure to some children. Instead, keep a small amount available alongside something familiar, and allow the child to explore it without being required to taste it. For your child: Offer meals and snacks at reasonably predictable times, and try to include at least one familiar food without making a separate made-to-order meal. If very little is eaten, end the meal calmly and offer the next planned meal or snack at the usual time. Use judgement if your child is unwell, unusually hungry, has feeding difficulties or has been advised to follow a different routine.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idiyappam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idiyappam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of dry ginger (saunth) powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal and a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Food, love and family tradition", "notes": "Food is often an expression of care in Indian families — an elder wanting to see a plate finished, or a laddoo offered as encouragement, usually comes from love and a real memory of scarcity, not from wanting to create pressure. The underlying science of how a toddler''s appetite and food preferences form is the same everywhere; how a family chooses to honour that science while keeping its own traditions of care and elder involvement intact is every family''s own call, not a single ''correct'' way to feed a child. Children typically need to meet a new or disliked food more than once before it becomes familiar — there''s no fixed number that applies to every child or every food, so a food given up on after one or two tries may simply need more time. In one feeding study, children told a food was given ''just because,'' with no strings attached, rated it as more likeable over repeated tastes than children given the exact same food as a reward for eating something else — suggesting how a food is framed can shape a child''s preference for it, not just the tasting itself.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana, softened or crushed appropriately for the toddler, with milk or curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Hummus or a smooth chana spread with soft roti strips or toast, with the toddler''s portion modified for safe chewing", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal and boneless, finely shredded chicken", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising a calmer table", "notes": "Today is not a test. Choose one small change: serve a modest first portion, include one familiar food, or let the meal end without discussing how much was eaten. Did any meal become a negotiation this week? Is there one gentle shift—removing the dessert bargain, offering a smaller first portion, or allowing the plate to end without comment—that you would like to continue?", "breakfast_a": "Suji cheela with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked mixed vegetables, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked mixed vegetables, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft seasonal fruit, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, with soft fruit, finely chopped", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'None of this requires perfect follow-through starting tonight. Small, steady shifts — a meal that ends without pressure, a dessert served without conditions, a vegetable that quietly reappears without comment — do more for a toddler''s relationship with food over time than any single meal, finished or not.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
