-- postpartum week 108: Juice, flavoured milk and "health drink" powders: rethinking your toddler's sweet drinks
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   108,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Juice, flavoured milk and "health drink" powders: rethinking your toddler''s sweet drinks',
   '"A toddler''s stomach is small, and a sweet drink can reduce their appetite for the food offered at a meal. Flavoured milk and many malted drink powders contain added sugar, while even 100% fruit juice contributes free sugar and lacks the fibre of whole fruit. Water between meals and an appropriate amount of plain milk are the usual everyday choices; juice is optional, not required."',
   'Fruit juice contains free sugar even when no sugar has been added. Flavoured milk and many malted drink powders may contain added sugar. These drinks therefore deserve the same label awareness and portion attention as other sweet foods and drinks. Each day this week unpacks one part of that idea in more detail. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household feels strongly about a daily glass of a malted drink: there''s no need for a confrontation — a smaller glass offered occasionally rather than daily, or a plain-milk version without the sweetened powder, can meet everyone partway. If your toddler repeatedly eats very little, has difficulty chewing or swallowing, loses previously accepted foods, seems unusually tired, or you are concerned about growth, discuss it with their paediatric healthcare professional. Avoid using pressure as the solution. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: juice, flavoured milk and sweetened drink powders can affect glucose even when the front label says ''no added sugar.'' Include them in the carbohydrate plan already advised for you. If juice has been prescribed for treating low blood glucose, follow that medical plan. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs a dramatic change tonight — offering water first, keeping any juice within the age-appropriate maximum, or checking one familiar powder for added sugar is meaningful progress. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about sweet or free-sugar-containing drinks and labels applies regardless of what''s served. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fruit juice is not required. If 100% juice is offered to a child aged one to three, keep it to no more than 120 ml — about half a cup — in the entire day. This is a maximum, not a daily target. Whole fruit is preferred over juice because it keeps the fibre and takes longer to eat, while the same fruit''s sugar in juice form is finished in moments and doesn''t fill a toddler up the same way.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe guava (amrud) pulp, with firm seeds removed or strained where needed, served with finely ground nuts or seeds if already introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer cubes or crumbles with soft, ripe guava (amrud) pulp, with firm seeds removed or strained where needed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked egg bhurji and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why juice counts as sugar, even when it''s real fruit", "notes": "The sugars naturally present in fruit juice fall within the World Health Organization''s definition of free sugars, even when no sugar has been added during manufacturing. This differs from whole fruit, where the sugars remain within the fruit''s cellular structure and come with fibre. India''s 2024 dietary guidelines separately advise avoiding added sugar before age two and keeping it limited thereafter. A ''100% juice, no added sugar'' label may therefore be accurate, but it does not make juice equivalent to whole fruit or remove the need for portion limits.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated bottle gourd and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with grated bottle gourd and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft orange flesh, peeled, deseeded and finely broken into small pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and folded in", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "How a sweet drink is sipped matters as much as how much", "notes": "How a sweet drink is consumed matters as much as how much: sipping juice or sweetened milk slowly over a long stretch — from a cup carried around and revisited through the day — keeps a toddler''s mouth acidic for far longer than the same drink finished at one sitting, because saliva doesn''t get the same chance to clear the sugar in between sips. A cup a toddler carries through the day is best filled with plain water; keep juice, sweetened milk or any other sweet drink to mealtimes, when saliva flow is already higher.", "breakfast_a": "Savoury vegetable oats with grated paneer", "breakfast_b": "Savoury vegetable oats with a fully cooked scrambled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and a pinch of cardamom powder", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin powder", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a moong dal and boiled chickpeas (chole), lightly mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Reading past the promise on a health-drink label", "notes": "In April 2024, FSSAI clarified that ''health drink'' is not a defined or standardised category under Indian food law and asked e-commerce platforms to categorise products appropriately. Regulatory action does not automatically mean a product is banned or unsafe; it means that broad growth, immunity or health promises should not replace scrutiny of the ingredient list, sugar content and serving size. Plain milk does not require a commercial powder unless one has been recommended for a specific nutritional reason.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya upma with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, and soft-cooked potato (aloo), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked potato (aloo), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pinch of nutmeg powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower (gobi), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What to reach for instead", "notes": "Plain water between meals and an appropriate amount of plain milk are the usual everyday drinks for a toddler. Plain milk already supplies the nutrients expected from that milk serving; a commercial powder does not need to be added unless a particular supplement has been recommended for the child. Whole fruit is preferable to fruit juice. If juice is offered, measure the actual juice quantity, keep it within the age-appropriate maximum, and serve it in a cup with a meal rather than allowing it to be carried and sipped throughout the day. For your child: A favourite sweet drink does not have to become the centre of a conflict. Offer water first, keep sweetened drinks occasional, and avoid using them as a substitute for a missed meal. If 100% juice is served, offer a small measured amount with a meal. Adding water changes its concentration but does not erase the sugar already present in the juice.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paneer paratha with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paneer paratha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked peas (matar), lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked peas (matar), lightly mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet potato (shakarkandi) with a small bowl of curd and a pinch of roasted cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer, a squeeze of lemon and a pinch of roasted cumin, without added black salt", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), mashed", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Food, love and marketing", "notes": "Offering a favourite sweet drink usually comes from real care — a grandparent''s insistence on a daily glass of a malted drink, or a parent''s own memory of growing up on one, reflects love and a genuine belief that it helps a child grow, not an attempt to overindulge. Decades of marketing have leaned hard on that instinct. None of this makes the choice wrong for your family — it means the decision is worth making with the same information you''d use for any other sweetened food, rather than assuming the promise on the label is settled science. Indian food law distinguishes fruit juice, nectar and squash according to their composition. The names should not be treated as interchangeable: nectars and squashes generally contain less fruit and may contain added sugar. Read the ingredient list and nutrition panel rather than relying only on fruit pictures or front-label wording. In June 2024, FSSAI directed food businesses to remove ''100% fruit juice'' claims from reconstituted fruit juices. This is a labelling and advertising rule; it does not turn juice into a required toddler drink or make the front label a substitute for reading the ingredients.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with peanuts used only if already introduced and tolerated; ground finely or as a smooth peanut paste in the toddler''s portion, never whole peanuts", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and gently folded in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising label literacy", "notes": "Today is not about strict rules. Choose one small shift: check one drink''s label at home, offer water first when a sweet drink is asked for, or notice how a favourite drink is actually being sipped through the day. Did a sweet drink show up more than expected this week? Is there one small shift — keeping the actual juice portion small, serving it with a meal, or looking twice at a health-drink label — worth continuing?", "breakfast_a": "Ragi cheela with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, with soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked potato (aloo), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and a fully cooked egg curry", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'None of this requires perfect follow-through starting tonight. Small, steady shifts — plain water offered first, a favourite drink kept to mealtimes, one label read a little more closely — do more for a toddler''s relationship with sweet drinks over time than any single rule enforced all at once.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 109: Texture and sensory sensitivities: when your toddler resists lumps, mixed textures, or certain mouthfeels
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   109,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Texture and sensory sensitivities: when your toddler resists lumps, mixed textures, or certain mouthfeels',
   '"A toddler may reject a food because of how it feels in the mouth rather than how it tastes. Gentle exposure can help some children become more familiar with manageable textures, but pressure is unlikely to help. If texture restriction is persistent, affects nutrition, or comes with difficulty chewing or swallowing, it deserves professional assessment rather than increasingly challenging practice at home."',
   'By this stage, many toddlers manage a range of modified family-food textures, but development varies. Some avoid particular textures because they feel unfamiliar or unpleasant; others may have oral-motor, swallowing, pain-related or developmental factors affecting eating. Offer manageable variation without pressure, but do not assume every persistent texture restriction is an ordinary phase. The child''s safety, nutrition and overall feeding experience matter more than meeting a texture checklist. Each day this week unpacks one part of that idea in more detail. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household is worried about your toddler refusing certain textures: explain that children vary in how they respond to food textures. Keep mealtimes calm and avoid pressure, while paying attention to whether the restriction is persistent, affects nutrition or comes with chewing or swallowing difficulty. If your toddler repeatedly coughs or chokes during meals, has a wet or gurgly voice after swallowing, struggles to breathe, holds food in the mouth, takes an unusually long time to chew, repeatedly vomits with textures, loses accepted foods, or has restricted intake affecting growth or nutrition, discuss it promptly with their paediatric healthcare professional. Assessment may involve a feeding team such as a speech-language pathologist, occupational therapist and dietitian. Choking or difficulty breathing is an emergency. Gagging and choking are not the same. Gagging is usually noisy and is a protective reflex, although frequent gagging that prevents texture progression still deserves attention. Choking may be quiet and can involve inability to breathe, cry or cough effectively. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires a dramatic change tonight. One familiar food kept available, one safely manageable texture offered separately, and careful attention to how your child chews and swallows are enough for this week. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about textures and gentle exposure applies regardless of what''s served. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- How to use texture exposure this week: Keep a familiar, safely manageable food available. If your child is comfortable, place a very small amount of one slightly different—but still safe—texture separately on the plate. They may look, touch, smell, lick, taste or ignore it. Do not mix it secretly into a preferred food, insist on a bite, or progress to harder textures when chewing or swallowing is uncertain.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "By around 12 months, many children can eat the same basic foods as the family when the food is modified for safety and their abilities. At two years, progress is better judged by function—whether the child can comfortably chew and swallow a useful variety—than by a rigid list of textures. Hard, round, sticky or slippery foods can remain choking hazards and should never be used as chewing practice.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) with grated carrot and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Deseeded watermelon prepared in thin, manageable slices or another shape appropriate to the toddler''s chewing ability", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of coriander powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked egg bhurji and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why texture resistance is its own pattern, not pickiness", "notes": "Rejecting food because of how it feels in the mouth is a recognised pattern, separate from disliking how something tastes — and it works by a different mechanism than the food-specific pickiness this series has covered before. Texture avoidance can reflect a strong preference or sensory sensitivity, but similar behaviour can also occur with oral-motor, swallowing, pain-related or developmental differences. The behaviour alone cannot identify the cause. Look at the full picture: safety, variety, comfort, mealtime length and whether nutrition is being affected.", "breakfast_a": "Soft paneer-and-vegetable toast fingers, prepared in a manageable texture", "breakfast_b": "Fully cooked vegetable omelette strips with soft toast fingers", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot (chukandar), finely grated", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked beetroot (chukandar), finely grated", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet-potato wedges with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft paneer or tofu strips with ripe seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Small, related steps — bridging a new texture to a familiar one", "notes": "One approach used in feeding therapy for building texture tolerance is offering a new texture alongside a food already accepted, in small, related steps — a plain cracker your toddler already eats, for example, next to a very similar cracker with a thin layer of something spread on it, rather than jumping straight to something entirely different. This everyday, low-pressure approach can be tried at home without special training; the evidence behind it is more descriptive than rigorously tested, but the underlying idea — small, related steps rather than a big jump — is easy to apply.", "breakfast_a": "Khara pongal with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Khara pongal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Very ripe pineapple with the fibrous core removed, prepared in thin, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of cumin-coriander powder", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and boiled chickpeas (chole), lightly mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Practice away from the plate, and everyday chewing practice", "notes": "Optional, supervised exploration—touching, squashing or smelling a safe food without being expected to eat it—may help some children become more familiar with a texture, although the evidence is still limited. Use only foods the child could safely place in their mouth, because food play can quickly become eating. Safe family foods provide everyday chewing experience, but harder or more resistant foods should not be used as oral-motor exercises unless recommended after assessment.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paratha with mixed vegetables and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Stuffed paratha with mixed vegetables and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar)", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with rajma, cooked until very soft and lightly mashed, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar)", "nourishment_break_a": "Seedless grapes, quartered lengthwise, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What actually helps", "notes": "Offering a familiar texture at most meals, alongside one slightly different item with no expectation it gets eaten, keeps a toddler''s plate approachable while still building exposure. There''s no need to hide a disliked texture inside a preferred one to get it eaten — plain, honest exposure over time, without pressure or a reward attached, is what this series has already covered for picky eating generally, and it applies here too. For your child: A toddler who spits out a new texture, or eats around it, is still learning from having it on the plate — that''s not a failed attempt, even when it feels that way. Keep offering, keep it low-key, and let your child set the pace.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dhokla with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava dhokla with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and raw banana (kacha kela), soft-cooked and mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and raw banana (kacha kela), soft-cooked and mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed, with soft toast fingers", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of dried mint powder", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), mashed", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Food, culture and what''s already on the plate", "notes": "Indian family food naturally offers many textures: thin dal, soft rice, dry sabzi, idli, roti, dosa edges and mixed dishes. Texture progression can therefore use foods the family already eats rather than following a foreign-looking list of purées and finger foods. Guidance recommends introducing increasingly varied consistencies during complementary feeding, adapted to the child''s abilities. Some observational research has associated delayed introduction of lumpy foods beyond roughly 9–10 months with later feeding difficulty, but this does not prove cause or mean a parent created the problem. Support can still be useful whenever a concern becomes apparent.", "breakfast_a": "Soft vegetable thepla strips with curd", "breakfast_b": "Soft vegetable thepla strips with fully cooked, finely shredded chicken", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe pear slices with a very thin layer of smooth nut or seed butter, only if already introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "Smooth hummus with soft roti strips", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising gentle texture exposure", "notes": "Today is not about getting your toddler to eat a new texture. Choose one small step: offer a familiar food in a slightly different texture, let your toddler touch or play with something new away from the table, or simply notice which textures come up again and again this week. Did a particular texture show up as a sticking point this week? Is there one small step — a familiar food offered alongside something slightly different, or a few minutes of no-pressure food play — worth continuing?", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Oats idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "nourishment_break_a": "Sweet lime flesh with all seeds and tough membranes removed, separated into manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and mixed through", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'None of this requires perfect follow-through starting tonight. Small, steady shifts — a familiar texture kept on the plate, a new one offered without pressure, a few minutes of touching and playing with food away from mealtime — do more for a toddler''s relationship with texture over time than any single meal, accepted or not.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
