  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 110: How sleep affects your toddler's appetite and eating
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   110,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How sleep affects your toddler''s appetite and eating',
   '"Sleep and eating are connected, but not in one predictable direction. After poor or insufficient sleep, some toddlers may become too tired or unsettled to manage a meal comfortably, while others may show greater food responsiveness or eat when they are not truly hungry. One difficult night or meal is not a diagnosis; patterns over time matter more."',
   'Toddlers this age typically need about 11 to 14 hours of sleep in 24 hours, including a nap — a useful expert-consensus range rather than an exact target every child must hit. Sleep and eating are genuinely connected, but research doesn''t point in one single direction: some toddlers eat less or struggle to manage a meal after poor sleep, while others show more interest in food or eat past fullness. Both patterns are real, and neither is a diagnosis on its own — what matters more is your own toddler''s pattern over time, not a single hard night or difficult meal. Sleep and eating can also share an underlying cause, worth mentioning to your paediatrician if either persists. Each day this week unpacks one part of that idea in more detail. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household attributes your toddler''s poor eating to being spoiled or difficult: gently explain that sleep can influence how some toddlers manage meals, but one difficult meal does not reveal the cause. Keep the response calm, notice whether a pattern repeats, and consider a steadier bedtime or wind-down routine if tiredness regularly seems to be involved. If your toddler snores loudly, seems to gasp or pause breathing during sleep, shows restlessness or leg discomfort at night, or has poor sleep and poor appetite together with slow weight gain, mention it to their paediatrician — this combination can point to a treatable cause such as obstructive sleep apnoea or low iron, rather than an ordinary phase. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires a perfect bedtime or a perfect meal tonight. Noticing whether tiredness, not food, is behind a hard mealtime is often the more useful first step. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about sleep and appetite applies regardless of what''s served. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Toddlers between one and two years old typically need about 11 to 14 hours of sleep across a 24-hour day, including a daytime nap; by three to five years, this eases to roughly 10 to 13 hours. These figures come from an expert-consensus process built on a fairly limited body of paediatric sleep research, so treat them as a useful general range rather than an exact target every child must hit. A toddler who sleeps a little outside this range, but wakes rested and eats and plays normally through the day, is not necessarily a cause for concern.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apple, peeled and grated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of fennel (saunf) powder", "dinner_a": "Savoury vegetable oats with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "dinner_b": "Savoury vegetable oats with a fully cooked scrambled egg mixed through", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why sleep and appetite are connected", "notes": "In adults, short sleep is well documented to disrupt the hormones that regulate hunger and fullness. Whether the same mechanism works the same way in toddlers is genuinely unclear — it hasn''t been closely studied in young children, and the little research that exists is mixed. What''s better supported is a behavioural association: a few studies have found that toddlers who sleep less, or less consistently, are more likely to eat past fullness or show stronger interest in food regardless of hunger. These are observational findings, not proof that poor sleep causes this pattern — routine, stress and other factors could be involved too — but the link between sleep and eating behaviour at this age is real enough to take seriously.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dosa with soft paneer cubes on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft idiyappam with a mild vegetable stew", "lunch_b": "Soft idiyappam with a mild vegetable stew and a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry stirred through, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet corn kernels, mashed, with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of ajwain (carom seed) powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When tiredness changes the mealtime", "notes": "An overtired toddler doesn''t always eat less — some do, but others become more distracted, more easily upset, or more inclined to graze or snack rather than sit through a full meal. What''s more consistently observed is that tiredness changes how a toddler manages a meal: it becomes harder for them to self-regulate, sit still, or notice fullness cues, in either direction. Rather than assuming a difficult meal is only about the food, or that a tired child will simply eat less, it''s worth watching for your own toddler''s particular pattern.", "breakfast_a": "Suji uttapam with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with ripe muskmelon (kharbuja), deseeded and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a squeeze of lime", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "When it''s not just tiredness: iron and sleep", "notes": "Poor sleep and reduced appetite can sometimes occur alongside iron deficiency, but neither symptom is specific to iron status. Drinking large quantities of cow''s milk may crowd iron-rich foods out of a toddler''s diet. If poor sleep or poor eating persists—particularly alongside pallor, unusual tiredness, slow growth, pica or marked restlessness—mention the full pattern to your child''s paediatrician. They can review the child''s diet, growth and symptoms and decide whether testing is appropriate.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd and finely shredded, fully cooked chicken on the side", "lunch_a": "Mashed rajma (kidney beans), well-cooked and mashed smooth, with soft rice and a small bowl of curd", "lunch_b": "Mashed rajma (kidney beans), well-cooked and mashed smooth, with soft rice and mashed ripe avocado", "nourishment_break_a": "Dried dates (khajoor), soaked until very soft, then mashed into a smooth paste and stirred through a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped and stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What actually helps", "notes": "A steady bedtime and a calm, familiar wind-down routine are associated with better sleep in young children. If tiredness repeatedly seems to make dinner difficult, adjusting the timing or complexity of the evening meal may be worth trying. There is no universally recommended gap between dinner and bedtime; observe what helps your own child eat and settle comfortably. For your child: A toddler who''s had a rough night doesn''t need to prove anything at the table the next day. A smaller, simpler meal and an earlier bedtime are enough for one off day — no need to fix everything at once.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya (broken wheat) with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable daliya (broken wheat) with soft paneer cubes on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "Finely powdered roasted chana (bhuna chana) stirred thoroughly into a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a squeeze of lemon", "dinner_a": "Vegetable handvo, softened if needed, with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_b": "Vegetable handvo, softened if needed, with a small bowl of curd and a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Sleep, culture and family life", "notes": "Sleep timing and sleeping arrangements vary considerably across families and cultures. Older cross-national surveys reported later bedtimes and more room-sharing or bed-sharing in several Asian samples, including families in India. However, these were parent-reported convenience samples and should not be treated as a standard for every Indian household—or as proof that a later bedtime is automatically harmful. What matters more is whether the child receives broadly sufficient sleep across 24 hours and is generally alert, growing and functioning well. Children stop napping at widely varying ages. During a transition, a child may nap on some days and not others, which can affect mood, behaviour and sleep pressure. A missed nap does not produce the same nighttime response in every child. Look at total sleep across 24 hours and how your toddler functions during the day rather than forcing a particular nap schedule.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Sabudana khichdi with roasted peanuts, ground finely or as a smooth paste, used only if already introduced and tolerated, never whole", "lunch_a": "Vegetable-and-paneer appe with a mild vegetable sambar", "lunch_b": "Vegetable-and-paneer appe with a mild vegetable sambar and fully cooked, finely shredded chicken on the side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with kiwi, peeled and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pinch of amchur (dry mango powder)", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, soft-cooked chickpeas (chole), thoroughly mashed so no whole chickpeas remain, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising it this week", "notes": "Today is not about a perfect bedtime or a perfect meal. If a meal is difficult, pause before trying to solve it immediately. Could tiredness be contributing—or might hunger, discomfort, distraction, unfamiliar food or another factor be involved? You do not need to identify the cause from one meal. Notice what happened and look for patterns over time. Did sleep or tiredness appear to influence any of this week''s meals? Is there one small, low-pressure experiment—such as a calmer wind-down, an adjusted dinner time or a simpler meal on a very tired evening—worth observing next week?", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked peas (matar), lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked peas (matar), lightly mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe custard apple (sitaphal) pulp, seeds fully removed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped and mixed in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'None of this requires perfect follow-through starting tonight. Small, steady experiments—a calmer wind-down, noticing tiredness before a meal becomes difficult, or adjusting dinner timing when practical—may help you understand your toddler''s pattern. Keep what helps and release what does not.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 111: Should your toddler take probiotics? Curd, fermented foods and gut health
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   111,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Should your toddler take probiotics? Curd, fermented foods and gut health',
   '"A daily probiotic supplement has not been shown to be necessary for the routine general wellness of an otherwise healthy toddler — and that is reassuring, not a gap to fill. Some probiotics have specific clinical uses, but those findings cannot be transferred to every strain, product or child."',
   'Probiotics are live microorganisms shown, in a specific dose and strain, to provide a proven health benefit — a narrower definition than many products on the shelf imply. For a healthy toddler who isn''t sick or on antibiotics, no major paediatric body currently recommends routine daily probiotic supplementation for general health, immunity, mood or allergy prevention; the evidence behind most marketed claims is thin, mixed or simply hasn''t been studied in toddlers this age. Fermented foods already in many Indian kitchens — curd, idli and dosa among them — are valuable in their own right for taste, digestibility and nutrition, without needing to meet that stricter "proven probiotic" bar. For an otherwise healthy toddler, many commonly studied probiotics appear to be low-risk, but low-risk does not mean universally appropriate or necessary — effects and safety depend on the organism, product quality, dose and the child''s health. Each day this week unpacks one part of that idea in more detail. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household feels that your toddler needs a probiotic product for everyday digestion: gently explain that routine probiotic supplementation is not generally recommended for an otherwise healthy toddler. Curd or yogurt may contain live cultures when it has not been heat-treated after fermentation. Idli, dosa and similar foods are made through fermentation, but cooking means the finished food should not be described as a source of live bacteria. These foods remain valuable for their taste, texture and nutrition. Speak to your child''s paediatrician before using a probiotic if your child is currently immunocompromised, critically or seriously ill, has a central venous catheter, short-bowel syndrome or another complex medical or intestinal condition. A history of premature birth alone does not necessarily create the same risk once a child is older and otherwise healthy. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires a dramatic change tonight. Continue offering the varied foods your family already enjoys. An otherwise healthy toddler does not need a probiotic product simply because the label promises general digestive or immune support. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about probiotics and fermented foods applies regardless of what''s served. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "\"Probiotic,\" \"prebiotic\" and \"fermented food\" get used almost interchangeably in marketing, but they mean different things. A probiotic, by the standard scientific definition, is a live microorganism given in a specific, adequate amount that has been shown to produce a proven health benefit — not just \"contains bacteria.\" A fermented food, like curd or idli batter, is made through microbial fermentation and offers real nutritional value, but it doesn''t have to meet that same proof-of-benefit bar to be called \"fermented.\" Keeping these two ideas separate makes it much easier to judge what a product label is actually claiming.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi idli with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked egg bhurji and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Does a healthy toddler need a supplement?", "notes": "For a healthy toddler who isn''t sick and isn''t taking antibiotics, no major paediatric body currently recommends a daily probiotic supplement for general health. Reviews of the evidence in generally healthy children haven''t found enough support to recommend routine use, and no major paediatric gastroenterology society has issued guidance recommending probiotics for everyday wellness in children who aren''t unwell. This isn''t the same as saying probiotics never help with anything — some specific strains have shown benefit for specific problems — but \"give your healthy toddler a daily probiotic\" isn''t backed the way a supplement aisle display might suggest.", "breakfast_a": "Appam with a mild vegetable stew", "breakfast_b": "Appam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable poha, softened as needed, with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_b": "Vegetable poha, softened as needed, with a fully cooked scrambled egg mixed through", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe chikoo, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of dry ginger (saunth) powder", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot (chukandar), finely grated", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked beetroot (chukandar), finely grated", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What the marketing claims actually hold up", "notes": "Common claims on toddler probiotic packaging — that it \"boosts immunity,\" \"supports mood,\" or \"prevents allergies\" — mostly outpace the evidence available in healthy toddlers. The immunity and mood claims rely mainly on early, preliminary or adult research rather than solid evidence in young children. One older international allergy guideline conditionally suggested certain probiotic approaches during pregnancy, breastfeeding or early infancy for families whose infants had a high allergy risk, with the possible benefit relating mainly to eczema — but the certainty of evidence was very low. This is not the same as proving that probiotic products broadly \"prevent allergies,\" and it does not provide a reason to begin routine supplementation in an otherwise healthy two-year-old.", "breakfast_a": "Khaman dhokla with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Khaman dhokla with finely crumbled paneer on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), pulp scraped from the fibrous pod", "nourishment_break_a": "Unsweetened lassi made from pasteurised curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft, ripe kiwi, peeled and finely chopped, served with soft toast fingers spread very thinly with smooth almond butter, only if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "The everyday food story: curd, idli and dosa", "notes": "Curd, idli and dosa are all made using fermentation, but they are not microbiologically identical when eaten. Curd or yogurt may retain live cultures if it has not been heat-treated after culturing — though those cultures shouldn''t automatically be called probiotics unless the microorganism, viable amount and health benefit are appropriately established. Idli and dosa batter is fermented, but the food is then steamed or cooked, so the finished idli or dosa should not be presented as containing live cultures. Fermentation can still influence flavour, texture and digestibility, and these foods remain nutritious without needing a probiotic claim.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with finely crumbled paneer on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava (amrud) pulp, with firm seeds removed or strained where needed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pinch of cardamom powder", "dinner_a": "Soft millet-and-vegetable pulao with finely crumbled paneer", "dinner_b": "Soft millet-and-vegetable pulao with a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, stirred through", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What actually helps", "notes": "If you''re weighing whether to buy a probiotic supplement or gummy for your toddler, the honest answer is that it''s unlikely to be necessary for a healthy child — and the supplement industry has real, documented problems with mislabelled strains and inconsistent dosing, so a product doesn''t always reliably contain what the label claims. A varied diet that includes fermented foods your family already eats is a reasonable, evidence-grounded foundation on its own. If you do want to try a supplement, it''s worth discussing with your paediatrician first, and treating it as an extra rather than a replacement for ordinary good nutrition. For your child: None of this is about withholding something your toddler needs. A generally healthy toddler eating a varied diet isn''t missing out by not taking a probiotic supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idiyappam with finely crumbled paneer on the side", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idiyappam with fully cooked, finely shredded chicken on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft lemon rice with a mild vegetable raita", "lunch_b": "Soft lemon rice with a mild vegetable raita and boneless, finely shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "Plain, unsalted buttermilk (chaas) made from pasteurised or previously boiled milk, with soft roti torn into manageable strips", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Who probiotics carry real risk for", "notes": "Probiotic-associated infections are rare in healthy children, but documented cases cluster in specific higher-risk groups: medically fragile premature infants, children who are immunocompromised or critically ill, those with a central venous catheter, and children with short-bowel syndrome or another complex intestinal condition. A history of premature birth alone, without an ongoing complex medical condition, does not carry the same risk once a child is older and otherwise healthy. Worth knowing: Some probiotics have been studied for particular paediatric conditions, but recommendations are strain-specific and condition-specific. Evidence for one organism in one clinical situation cannot be transferred to another product or used as a reason for routine daily supplementation. Probiotics are not recommended as a reliable treatment for functional constipation in children. In India, FSSAI regulations define requirements for probiotic foods, including strain identification, viable numbers, serving information, storage conditions and substantiation of probiotic properties. Meeting regulatory and labelling requirements does not mean that every product has been shown to improve general health in every child — parents still need to look for evidence relating to the particular strain, dose, age group and intended use. FSSAI also states that ordinary starter cultures in dahi are not considered probiotics unless their probiotic properties have been substantiated.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Neer dosa with finely crumbled paneer on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable oats uttapam with a small bowl of curd", "lunch_b": "Vegetable oats uttapam with a small bowl of curd and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed sweet potato with a small bowl of curd and finely ground sesame seeds, only if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of asafoetida (hing) and roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising it this week", "notes": "Today is not about deciding whether to buy a supplement. Notice which fermented foods are already part of your family''s meals — while remembering that fermented does not automatically mean probiotic. For an otherwise healthy toddler''s everyday wellness, there is no need to add a probiotic product simply to make the diet feel more complete. A probiotic recommended for a specific medical situation is a separate decision. Did this week change how you think about a probiotic product you''d seen advertised? Is there a fermented food your family already eats regularly that''s doing more good than you''d realised?", "breakfast_a": "Plain idli with a mild vegetable sambar", "breakfast_b": "Plain idli with a mild vegetable sambar and finely crumbled paneer on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange flesh, peel, seeds and tough membrane removed, finely chopped into manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed and mixed through", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, extra grated paneer and soft-cooked cabbage (patta gobi), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'A varied diet that includes foods your family already enjoys is a sound foundation for an otherwise healthy toddler. Routine probiotic supplementation is not required for general wellness. If a probiotic is being considered for a particular illness or treatment situation, discuss the specific strain, dose and evidence with your child''s healthcare professional.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
