-- postpartum week 113: Screens at mealtimes: what they do to a toddler's eating, and how to get through a meal without one
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   113,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Screens at mealtimes: what they do to a toddler''s eating, and how to get through a meal without one',
   '"A meal without a screen doesn''t have to be peaceful to count as a success — it just has to be short, calm, and allowed to end."',
   'Using a screen to get through a meal is extremely common, and it is not a sign of bad parenting. It is still worth understanding what the evidence suggests. Experimental research conducted mainly in older children and adults has found that television during eating may produce a small increase in food intake, with a possible larger effect at a later eating occasion, but the findings are heterogeneous and should not be treated as direct toddler evidence. Observational studies in young children have also associated mealtime screens with less healthy dietary patterns, although they cannot show that the screen itself caused those patterns. The Indian Academy of Pediatrics advises against using screens to facilitate feeding and recommends screen-free mealtimes. The practical goal is therefore not a perfect family meal, but a predictable, responsive eating opportunity in which the child can notice food, family interaction and their own hunger and fullness cues. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household insists a screen or the TV is the only way to get your toddler to eat: you can explain that screens are not recommended as a routine feeding tool. They may distract a child into continuing a meal, but that does not reliably improve dietary quality or help the child recognise hunger and fullness cues. Offer regular meals and snacks in a calm setting, allow the child to decide whether and how much to eat from what is offered, and follow their signals that the meal is finished. A child who is losing weight, showing faltering growth or dropping across growth percentiles, coughing or choking during meals, taking unusually long to eat, refusing most foods or showing another feeding difficulty needs individualised paediatric assessment. If your toddler will only eat when a screen is on, refuses to eat at all without one, or mealtimes have become a consistent source of family conflict, talk to their paediatrician or a feeding specialist about a gradual plan — this is a genuinely common, manageable pattern, not a sign that something has gone permanently wrong. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires an overnight household rule change. One screen-free meal this week is plenty to start with. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. The guidance about screens and mealtimes applies regardless of what''s served. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Using screens during meals is common, but prevalence estimates vary considerably by the child''s age, country and the way researchers define mealtime screen exposure. The Indian Academy of Pediatrics recommends avoiding screens below age two and limiting supervised screen time to no more than one hour a day from 24 to 59 months. It also advises that screens should not be used to facilitate feeding. WHO similarly recommends no more than one hour of sedentary screen time at age two, with less being better. An occasional difficult meal does not undo a family''s efforts; the aim is to make screen-free eating the usual routine. Now that your toddler is two, the guidance shifts from avoiding screens to limiting them: no more than one supervised hour a day, with less being better, while keeping meals screen-free where possible.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with arhar dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with arhar dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed avocado and chickpea spread on soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What the evidence actually shows", "notes": "A 2025 review pooled 57 effect sizes from 23 experimental studies across children and adults. When all included comparisons were considered, it did not find a clear overall effect and the results varied substantially. In an analysis restricted to television viewing, it found a small increase in food intake, with a larger estimate at the next eating occasion. Only limited studies contributed to that later-intake finding, and most participants were older children or adults rather than toddlers. The review therefore supports avoiding distracted eating as a reasonable routine, but it does not prove that a screen will make every toddler overeat.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated bottle gourd and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with grated bottle gourd and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Sweet-potato and lentil patties, softened for the toddler, with a small bowl of curd", "lunch_b": "Sweet-potato and lentil patties, softened for the toddler, with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mango, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft moong-dal and vegetable mini cheela, cut into manageable strips, with curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked raw banana (kacha kela), mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked raw banana (kacha kela), mashed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The diet-quality signal", "notes": "Several observational studies in toddlers and young children have associated mealtime screen exposure with less healthy dietary patterns, including more sugary drinks, fast food or discretionary snacks and fewer fruits or vegetables. Findings are not completely uniform, however, and many studies rely on parent reports or cross-sectional data. Families who use screens during meals may also differ in other ways that influence the food served. The evidence therefore supports a screen-free default, but it does not justify blaming a screen — or a parent — for the nutritional quality of any single meal. This is about a pattern across many meals, not a verdict on any single dinner eaten in front of a show.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked french beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked french beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apple, peeled and grated", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed banana and cooked rolled oats stirred into a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why parents reach for a screen", "notes": "Qualitative research and clinical experience suggest several understandable reasons parents use screens during meals: calming an upset child, extending a difficult meal, managing food refusal or simply creating a few quiet minutes after a long day. None of that removes the possible effects on eating, but it does mean this is a habit to adjust gently rather than a failure worth feeling guilty about. Thoughtful, attentive parents can also find themselves relying on screens during difficult meals. Mealtime screen use has been reported across many different family and cultural settings. How common it is varies according to the child''s age, location and how screen exposure is measured.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe custard apple (sitaphal) pulp, seeds fully removed — check the pulp carefully, as small seed fragments may remain", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a squeeze of lemon", "dinner_a": "Vegetable daliya (broken wheat) khichdi with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_b": "Vegetable daliya (broken wheat) khichdi with a small bowl of curd and a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry stirred through, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A shorter, calmer meal", "notes": "Toddler meals do not need to last a long time. Around 20-30 minutes is usually enough, and some meals will naturally end sooner when the child consistently turns away, closes their mouth, pushes food aside or loses interest. Offer the food, sit together where possible and avoid pressure, bargaining or chasing the child with additional bites. The caregiver decides what food is offered, when and where; the child decides whether and how much to eat. If a meal ends with little eaten, calmly provide the next planned meal or snack rather than immediately replacing it with preferred snack food. If mealtimes feel quiet without a screen, brief conversation about the food, naming colours or textures on the plate, or allowing safe self-feeding can provide gentle interaction. Simply ending the meal calmly is also a reasonable choice. For your child: If your toddler still wants to leave before finishing, that''s normal at this age — appetite genuinely varies day to day, and another chance to eat comes soon.", "breakfast_a": "Idiyappam with a mild vegetable stew", "breakfast_b": "Idiyappam with a mild vegetable stew and boneless, finely shredded chicken stirred through", "lunch_a": "Mild vegetable sambar with mini idli", "lunch_b": "Mild vegetable sambar with mini idli and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon (kharbuja), deseeded and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft, ripe pear, peeled and grated, with finely crumbled paneer stirred through", "dinner_a": "Soft paneer-and-vegetable pulao", "dinner_b": "Soft vegetable pulao with tender, finely shredded mutton, fully cooked and free of bone fragments", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A screen-free eating spot", "notes": "Indian research has also documented frequent screen exposure in young children, including use during meals or feeding. Study designs and populations differ, so these findings should not be combined into one national prevalence estimate. The clearer practical guidance comes from the Indian Academy of Pediatrics: do not use screens to facilitate feeding, and keep mealtimes screen-free where possible. A simple, low-effort way to apply this: treat the eating spot itself — the highchair or the family table — as a screen-free zone, the same way a kitchen counter might be a no-shoes zone. Worth knowing: None of this is about removing screens from your child''s life altogether — it''s specifically about the eating moment itself, which is a much smaller, more manageable change.", "breakfast_a": "Suji uttapam with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked white radish (mooli), finely grated", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked white radish (mooli), finely grated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe sweet lime (mosambi), peeled, seeds and membrane removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of amchur (dry mango) powder", "dinner_a": "Vegetable appam with a mild lentil stew", "dinner_b": "Vegetable appam with a mild lentil stew and boneless, finely shredded chicken stirred through", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising it this week", "notes": "Try one mealtime this week — perhaps the one that currently relies on a screen most — without it, and see what actually happens. It may go more smoothly than expected, or it may be a genuinely hard ten minutes; either is useful information, not a verdict on your parenting. If it''s hard, a short, boring meal that ends on time is still a completely reasonable outcome. Did today''s screen-free meal feel different — easier, harder, or about the same as usual? Which mealtime this week would be worth trying it again?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, softened as needed, with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha, softened as needed, with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, mixed through", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with fresh, soft, ripe fig, stem removed, peeled if the skin is tough, and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked egg mashed with curd as a mild egg spread, served with a soft roti strip", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "still_hungry": "Choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or a small portion of the family meal."}]'::jsonb,
   'A screen-free meal doesn''t need to be a peaceful, lingering family moment — a short, ordinary meal without a screen is a reasonable routine to practise, even when it isn''t peaceful and even when your toddler eats less than you expected.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 114: When your toddler is gaining weight quickly: what's normal, what's worth asking about, and why not to restrict food
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   114,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'When your toddler is gaining weight quickly: what''s normal, what''s worth asking about, and why not to restrict food',
   '"A toddler''s body doesn''t need to be managed like a project — steady food, steady sleep, and room to grow at their own pace does the real work."',
   'If your toddler has been gaining weight faster than before, it''s worth understanding what actually matters and what doesn''t. From around age two, a clinician may begin using sex-specific BMI-for-age alongside height and weight trends, though the exact chart and cut-offs used depend on the country and clinical system — these shouldn''t be treated as interchangeable, and a growth chart is a screening tool, not a diagnosis. A noticeable change in trajectory may be worth reviewing, particularly when weight rises while height growth slows, but the meaning depends on the chart used, measurement accuracy and the child''s own pattern, not a single home measurement. Family history, sleep, sugary drinks and how food is offered all genuinely influence weight gain at this age, and a randomized US trial found that responsive parenting around feeding, sleep, play and comfort produced a modest reduction in average BMI by age three — though its overweight and obesity findings weren''t statistically conclusive. Major paediatric bodies are explicit that calorie restriction and weight-focused talk are not the recommended response for a toddler, since overt restriction is linked to less helpful eating patterns. The aim this week is steady, varied meals offered without pressure — not a number to chase or a food to fear. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household says a chubby toddler is a healthy toddler and encourages second helpings: you can gently explain that steady growth in both height and weight is what actually matters, not extra helpings — and that paediatric guidance specifically advises against restricting or pushing food either way for a toddler this age. Most toddlers regulate their own intake well when meals are offered without pressure in either direction; a child whose weight is climbing much faster than their height, or who has other symptoms alongside rapid weight gain, needs individualised paediatric assessment rather than a change made at home. If your toddler''s growth trajectory has changed noticeably — especially if weight is rising while height growth has slowed — or if you''re simply unsure what a home measurement means, bring it up with their paediatrician directly, who can interpret the actual chart alongside your child''s history; family history adds context but isn''t something to diagnose from at home, and this is a genuinely common, manageable conversation to have. Seek prompt medical advice if rapid weight change occurs alongside excessive thirst or urination, persistent constipation with unusual tiredness, reduced activity, long-term steroid use or another new symptom. Seek urgent medical care if your child has significant breathing difficulty, marked or rapidly developing swelling, unusual drowsiness, developmental regression or appears acutely unwell. These signs do not establish a diagnosis, but they should not be managed through dietary restriction at home. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires an overnight change to how your family eats. Offering the same varied meals, without added pressure in either direction, is plenty to start with. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. This week''s guidance is about how food is offered, not about restricting any particular meal or ingredient. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "From around age two, a clinician may begin using sex-specific BMI-for-age alongside height and weight trends, but the chart and cut-offs used depend on the country and clinical system. CDC charts classify BMI from the 85th to below the 95th percentile as overweight for children aged two and older. WHO standards for children under five use z-scores and commonly define overweight as weight-for-height above +2 standard deviations. These systems should not be treated as interchangeable. A growth chart is a screening tool, not a diagnosis, and should be interpreted by a trained health professional using repeated, accurately measured height and weight. A noticeable change in growth trajectory may be worth reviewing, particularly when weight or BMI rises while height growth slows — the meaning depends on the chart used, the accuracy of repeated measurements, the child''s previous pattern and their overall health, not one home measurement or percentile crossing alone. This week isn''t about hitting a target number; it''s about understanding what''s normal, what''s worth asking a paediatrician about, and why restricting a toddler''s food is not the recommended response.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with arhar dal and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with arhar dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed sweet potato with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What actually influences weight gain", "notes": "Several everyday factors genuinely influence how quickly a toddler gains weight. Family history and genetics play a substantial role — a child''s weight tends to track with their parents'' even accounting for a shared home environment. Shorter sleep in toddlers has been linked, across multiple studies, to a higher risk of excess weight gain over time. Sugary drinks show a consistent, modest association with weight gain across both observational and experimental research. And how food is offered matters too: regularly pressuring a child to finish everything on their plate is associated with a child eating past their own fullness cues, which can work against the appetite-regulation you actually want them to develop.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable handvo, softened as needed, with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable handvo, softened as needed, with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable pulao with a small bowl of plain curd on the side", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable pulao with finely crumbled paneer stirred through", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe kiwi, peeled and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed sweet corn kernels, mashed, with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What actually helps: the responsive-feeding evidence", "notes": "A randomized US trial called INSIGHT taught first-time parents responsive practices involving feeding, sleep, play and emotional regulation from infancy. At age three, children in the intervention group had a modestly lower average BMI z-score than the comparison group. Fewer children were classified as overweight or obese, but those particular differences were not statistically conclusive and could have occurred by chance. The study was conducted at one centre in a relatively homogeneous population, so it should not be treated as a guarantee for every family. It nevertheless supports a useful non-restrictive principle: respond to distress with comfort appropriate to the child''s need, maintain predictable sleep and meal routines, offer varied food repeatedly and avoid using food routinely as a reward or the only form of soothing.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava idli with finely crumbled paneer on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava (amrud), deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft chana-and-vegetable mini patties with curd", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why restriction backfires", "notes": "Overtly restricting food, making weight-based comments or tightly controlling how much a child is allowed to eat can increase preoccupation with restricted foods and may interfere with learning to respond to hunger and fullness. Studies have associated restrictive feeding with eating in the absence of hunger and other less helpful eating behaviours, but much of the evidence is observational and the relationship can work both ways — parents may also restrict more because a child already shows strong food responsiveness. In older children and adolescents, weight-focused dieting has been associated with later weight and eating problems. That evidence, together with concerns about growth and nutrient adequacy, is one reason unsupervised dieting or calorie restriction should not be introduced for a toddler. This is about long-term feeding patterns, not something your toddler — or you — did wrong, and it isn''t a reason to feel guilty about any single meal.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) with grated cabbage and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) with grated cabbage and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with moong dal and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange flesh, peel, seeds and tough membrane removed, finely chopped into manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of ajwain (carom seed) powder", "dinner_a": "Ragi roti with a mild mixed-vegetable curry", "dinner_b": "Ragi roti with a mild fish-and-vegetable curry, made with fully cooked, lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What guidelines actually recommend", "notes": "Indian Academy of Pediatrics guidance states that calorie restriction is not usually recommended before age six, with family-wide healthy routines, appropriate activity and weight maintenance generally preferred. This does not mean ignoring a clinically concerning growth pattern. A paediatrician may assess nutrition, sleep, activity, medications, development and possible medical contributors and may recommend individualized changes. Parents should not independently impose a weight-loss target or calorie-restricted diet on a toddler. In some children, a clinician may aim for slower weight gain while normal height growth continues. Whether that approach is appropriate depends on the child''s measurements, health and growth pattern. For your child: Keep weight categories, calorie language and body-focused comments out of conversations with your toddler. From their perspective, the routine should remain the same: varied family meals, calm eating opportunities and freedom to respond to hunger and fullness.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe jamun (Indian blackberry), stone removed and checked carefully for fragments of stone before mashing", "nourishment_break_b": "Grated paneer and finely mashed ripe banana mixed together", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and finely cooked mixed vegetables", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and finely cooked mixed vegetables If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The chubby-baby belief, and what''s actually worth watching", "notes": "The belief that a chubby baby is necessarily a healthy baby has been reported in parental research and often comes from care, family experience and a desire to protect the child from undernutrition. Body shape alone, however, cannot establish whether growth is healthy. NFHS-5 reported that 3.4% of Indian children under five met its weight-for-height definition of overweight in 2019-21, compared with 2.1% in NFHS-4 in 2015-16 — a substantial increase, though undernutrition remains far more prevalent, so both forms of malnutrition can exist within the same population. Most weight-gain patterns at this age are ordinary variation; what''s actually worth a paediatrician''s attention is weight climbing steadily while height growth stalls, assessed over time rather than from a single measurement or a family history alone. Worth knowing: None of this means treating your toddler differently at the table starting today — it means continuing the same responsive, non-restrictive approach this series has recommended all along.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Neer dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked carrot, finely grated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe dragon fruit (kamalam), mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a small pinch of cardamom powder", "dinner_a": "Set dosa with a mild vegetable curry", "dinner_b": "Set dosa with a mild vegetable-and-egg curry, fully cooked If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Practising it this week", "notes": "This week, if you''ve been worrying about your toddler''s weight, try one shift: instead of limiting a serving or commenting on how much they''ve eaten, focus on offering a variety of nutrient-dense foods, a consistent sleep routine, and letting your toddler decide how much of what''s offered to eat. If you have a specific concern about their growth trend, that''s worth bringing to their paediatrician directly, with the actual chart in front of you, rather than guessing from a single number at home.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi, cooked until completely soft and non-chewy with clumps separated before serving, with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Sabudana khichdi, cooked until completely soft and non-chewy with clumps separated before serving, with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe plum, peeled, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Fully cooked egg mashed with curd as a mild spread, served with soft roti strips", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Did thinking about your toddler''s weight this week change how you''d respond next time someone comments on it? What''s one thing you''d tell a paediatrician if you brought this up at your next visit?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Your toddler''s body is not a project to manage or a number to correct. Steady, varied food offered without pressure, appropriate sleep and active play support healthy growth. When the growth pattern raises concern, careful clinical assessment — not shame or home restriction — is the appropriate next step.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
